1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,169
At the ninth hour, nailed to a Roman cross

2
00:00:02,209 --> 00:00:04,907
and unable to fill his lungs, Jesus of Nazareth

3
00:00:04,948 --> 00:00:06,968
gathered his strength and cried out into the

4
00:00:06,989 --> 00:00:07,289
dark.

5
00:00:07,855 --> 00:00:11,369
"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?"

6
00:00:12,032 --> 00:00:14,853
We hear raw anguish a man forsaken.

7
00:00:15,903 --> 00:00:16,530
And it is that.

8
00:00:17,501 --> 00:00:18,694
But it is also a quotation.

9
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:21,672
Those exact words had been written down, in

10
00:00:21,692 --> 00:00:24,185
the first person, almost a thousand years before

11
00:00:24,225 --> 00:00:27,487
that morning by a king who died old and safe

12
00:00:27,668 --> 00:00:28,333
in his own bed.

13
00:00:28,333 --> 00:00:30,935
"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?

14
00:00:31,662 --> 00:00:33,898
why art thou so far from helping me, and from

15
00:00:33,925 --> 00:00:36,240
the words of my roaring?" This is Psalm twenty-two.

16
00:00:37,870 --> 00:00:39,643
And the dying Jesus did not reach for it by

17
00:00:39,643 --> 00:00:39,943
accident.

18
00:00:40,982 --> 00:00:43,235
He was telling everyone within earshot exactly

19
00:00:43,275 --> 00:00:43,718
where to look.

20
00:00:44,847 --> 00:00:46,038
King David wrote this song.

21
00:00:46,805 --> 00:00:51,610
He wrote it in the first person I, me, my as

22
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:52,981
if it were happening to his own body.

23
00:00:53,812 --> 00:00:56,393
But here is the strangeness: David himself

24
00:00:56,474 --> 00:00:57,903
never came near a death like the one he describes.

25
00:00:59,411 --> 00:01:01,707
Because what he describes is a public execution.

26
00:01:02,711 --> 00:01:05,528
A man stripped, surrounded, and mocked to death.

27
00:01:05,528 --> 00:01:08,519
"But I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of

28
00:01:08,559 --> 00:01:10,170
men, and despised of the people.

29
00:01:11,157 --> 00:01:13,279
All they that see me laugh me to scorn: they

30
00:01:13,319 --> 00:01:15,132
shoot out the lip, they shake the head…" And

31
00:01:15,201 --> 00:01:17,083
listen to what the mockers actually say: "He

32
00:01:17,124 --> 00:01:18,796
trusted on the LORD that he would deliver him:

33
00:01:19,465 --> 00:01:21,297
let him deliver him, seeing he delighted in

34
00:01:21,338 --> 00:01:23,540
him." David was a soldier and a king who died

35
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:25,758
of old age never stripped before a jeering

36
00:01:25,798 --> 00:01:27,386
crowd, never executed at all.

37
00:01:28,714 --> 00:01:30,768
So whose death is he describing in his own

38
00:01:30,788 --> 00:01:33,097
voice with such certainty that he writes it

39
00:01:33,128 --> 00:01:33,590
down as a fact?

40
00:01:34,982 --> 00:01:36,275
Then the song turns forensic.

41
00:01:36,761 --> 00:01:38,813
It stops sounding like poetry and starts sounding

42
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:40,392
like a witness statement from a cross that

43
00:01:40,432 --> 00:01:41,257
would not be invented for centuries.

44
00:01:41,821 --> 00:01:44,318
"I am poured out like water, and all my bones

45
00:01:44,363 --> 00:01:48,186
are out of joint…" The body of a man hung up

46
00:01:48,206 --> 00:01:50,680
by his arms the joints pulling apart under

47
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:51,020
his own weight.

48
00:01:51,004 --> 00:01:53,971
"…my tongue cleaveth to my jaws; and thou hast

49
00:01:54,011 --> 00:01:56,170
brought me into the dust of death." A killing

50
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:59,065
thirst on the cross, the apostle John records

51
00:01:59,065 --> 00:01:59,365
the cry: I thirst.

52
00:02:01,618 --> 00:02:03,693
Then the song says the thing it has no business

53
00:02:03,743 --> 00:02:05,625
knowing this early: "…they pierced my hands

54
00:02:05,706 --> 00:02:07,505
and my feet." Pierced hands and feet.

55
00:02:09,042 --> 00:02:11,053
David lived a thousand years before Rome turned

56
00:02:11,134 --> 00:02:13,390
crucifixion into a science; his own people

57
00:02:13,451 --> 00:02:14,499
never executed that way.

58
00:02:15,187 --> 00:02:16,900
There was no reason on earth for him to picture

59
00:02:16,927 --> 00:02:18,901
a man fixed to wood through his hands and feet

60
00:02:19,509 --> 00:02:22,004
yet there it is, in ink, centuries early.

61
00:02:23,051 --> 00:02:25,326
And it keeps going, down to the smallest detail:

62
00:02:25,326 --> 00:02:27,464
"They part my garments among them, and cast

63
00:02:27,505 --> 00:02:29,603
lots upon my vesture." Soldiers gambling for

64
00:02:29,644 --> 00:02:30,590
a dead man's clothes.

65
00:02:31,778 --> 00:02:33,612
Turn to the foot of the cross, and John tells

66
00:02:33,644 --> 00:02:35,145
you it happened: "…that the scripture might

67
00:02:35,186 --> 00:02:37,547
be fulfilled, which saith, They parted my raiment

68
00:02:37,588 --> 00:02:40,009
among them, and for my vesture they did cast

69
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:42,002
lots." The Roman soldiers gambling for his

70
00:02:42,042 --> 00:02:43,446
clothes were not following a script.

71
00:02:44,037 --> 00:02:45,169
They were fulfilling one anyway.

72
00:02:46,268 --> 00:02:48,672
Now an honest objection, and it deserves a

73
00:02:48,682 --> 00:02:50,203
straight answer, not a sales pitch.

74
00:02:51,195 --> 00:02:53,128
A careful reader will say: this is poetry.

75
00:02:53,574 --> 00:02:55,627
David is a man who reached for vivid images

76
00:02:55,969 --> 00:02:58,471
dogs, a lion when his enemies pressed him.

77
00:02:59,259 --> 00:03:02,016
And on that one word, pierced, there is a genuine

78
00:03:02,084 --> 00:03:04,809
scholarly dispute: the traditional Hebrew text

79
00:03:04,849 --> 00:03:07,270
can be read as like a lion at my hands and

80
00:03:07,310 --> 00:03:09,301
feet rather than they pierced.

81
00:03:10,147 --> 00:03:12,142
That is a real question, honestly raised.

82
00:03:12,712 --> 00:03:13,663
So concede it cleanly.

83
00:03:14,271 --> 00:03:16,384
Take pierced off the table entirely, just for

84
00:03:16,424 --> 00:03:16,786
a moment.

85
00:03:17,473 --> 00:03:19,226
You are still left with a man stripped and

86
00:03:19,267 --> 00:03:22,209
mocked to death, his bones out of joint, dying

87
00:03:22,249 --> 00:03:24,527
of thirst, his clothing gambled away by the

88
00:03:24,567 --> 00:03:26,945
people who killed him written first-person,

89
00:03:27,367 --> 00:03:28,916
by a king who suffered none of it.

90
00:03:30,025 --> 00:03:31,678
One disputed word does not carry that.

91
00:03:32,168 --> 00:03:33,058
The convergence does.

92
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:36,432
And on the word itself: the Greek Old Testament

93
00:03:36,653 --> 00:03:38,849
translated by Jewish scholars almost two centuries

94
00:03:38,889 --> 00:03:41,342
before Jesus was born already read it they

95
00:03:41,402 --> 00:03:42,522
dug, or pierced.

96
00:03:43,209 --> 00:03:45,122
This is not a Christian edit smuggled in after

97
00:03:45,142 --> 00:03:45,442
the fact.

98
00:03:46,109 --> 00:03:48,622
The reading was there, in Jewish hands, centuries

99
00:03:48,663 --> 00:03:49,944
before there was a cross to defend.

100
00:03:50,851 --> 00:03:52,723
David is describing a death that is not his

101
00:03:52,824 --> 00:03:53,124
own.

102
00:03:53,572 --> 00:03:55,685
Which brings us back to the cross, and to why

103
00:03:55,752 --> 00:03:57,606
that screamed cry matters so much.

104
00:03:58,393 --> 00:04:00,204
When a first-century Jew quoted the opening

105
00:04:00,224 --> 00:04:02,265
line of a psalm, he was rarely reaching for

106
00:04:02,305 --> 00:04:03,030
just one line.

107
00:04:03,797 --> 00:04:05,858
The most natural way to hear it was as a pointer

108
00:04:05,898 --> 00:04:08,372
to the whole song the first verse pulling the

109
00:04:08,372 --> 00:04:08,672
entire psalm into the room.

110
00:04:10,578 --> 00:04:16,219
So when Jesus cried "My God, my God, why hast

111
00:04:16,303 --> 00:04:19,097
thou forsaken me?" he was not merely voicing

112
00:04:19,137 --> 00:04:19,459
his pain.

113
00:04:20,225 --> 00:04:23,002
He was placing himself as the I of Psalm twenty-two.

114
00:04:23,792 --> 00:04:24,360
Every line.

115
00:04:25,029 --> 00:04:26,880
He was claiming the wounded hands as his own,

116
00:04:27,222 --> 00:04:29,864
and the gambled clothes as his own even as

117
00:04:29,904 --> 00:04:32,162
the soldiers below him divided them up, exactly

118
00:04:32,202 --> 00:04:33,200
as verse eighteen said they would.

119
00:04:34,374 --> 00:04:36,908
He took the whole song onto himself, out loud,

120
00:04:37,150 --> 00:04:37,931
in front of witnesses.

121
00:04:38,681 --> 00:04:41,432
He was telling us: this one is about me.

122
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:44,512
But most people stop reading Psalm twenty-two

123
00:04:44,592 --> 00:04:45,075
at the cross.

124
00:04:45,742 --> 00:04:47,857
And if you stop there, you miss the most important

125
00:04:47,857 --> 00:04:48,157
thing about it.

126
00:04:49,205 --> 00:04:50,758
The song does not end in a grave.

127
00:04:51,463 --> 00:04:54,937
Halfway through, it turns violently, gloriously

128
00:04:55,278 --> 00:04:56,483
from death to life.

129
00:04:57,428 --> 00:04:59,740
The same voice that cried "forsaken" suddenly

130
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:01,564
stands up in the middle of a worshipping crowd:

131
00:05:01,564 --> 00:05:03,305
"I will declare thy name unto my brethren:

132
00:05:04,012 --> 00:05:06,127
in the midst of the congregation will I praise

133
00:05:06,328 --> 00:05:07,707
thee." A dead man declares nothing.

134
00:05:08,795 --> 00:05:10,827
Yet the voice that cried "forsaken" now stands

135
00:05:10,887 --> 00:05:12,902
alive in the congregation, praising.

136
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:15,793
You could read that as mere rescue from the

137
00:05:15,854 --> 00:05:18,094
edge of death except that Jesus took this whole

138
00:05:18,175 --> 00:05:20,333
song onto himself, and the New Testament puts

139
00:05:20,373 --> 00:05:22,614
this very line, "I will declare thy name unto

140
00:05:22,634 --> 00:05:24,122
my brethren," in the mouth of the risen Christ.

141
00:05:25,790 --> 00:05:27,944
The forsaken man of verse one and the living

142
00:05:27,984 --> 00:05:30,244
worshipper are the same person, on the far

143
00:05:30,284 --> 00:05:32,386
side of death because God did not leave him

144
00:05:32,406 --> 00:05:34,106
in the dust: "…neither hath he hid his face

145
00:05:34,146 --> 00:05:36,911
from him; but when he cried unto him, he heard."

146
00:05:37,051 --> 00:05:39,131
And then the song throws its arms open to the

147
00:05:39,171 --> 00:05:41,330
entire world: "All the ends of the world shall

148
00:05:41,371 --> 00:05:43,765
remember and turn unto the LORD: and all the

149
00:05:43,805 --> 00:05:45,897
kindreds of the nations shall worship before

150
00:05:45,897 --> 00:05:48,095
thee." The psalm that opens with one forsaken

151
00:05:48,135 --> 00:05:50,277
man closes with the nations streaming home.

152
00:05:51,246 --> 00:05:52,839
The cross was never the end of the song.

153
00:05:53,129 --> 00:05:53,676
It was the hinge.

154
00:05:54,647 --> 00:05:56,400
Look at the very last line David writes.

155
00:05:57,065 --> 00:05:59,739
After the wounds, after the turn, the song

156
00:05:59,799 --> 00:06:01,596
closes on a note of something accomplished

157
00:06:02,099 --> 00:06:04,619
a work finished: "…he hath done this." A thousand

158
00:06:04,639 --> 00:06:06,841
years later, on the cross, the dying Jesus

159
00:06:06,901 --> 00:06:07,867
reaches the same note.

160
00:06:08,696 --> 00:06:12,086
He receives the vinegar, and says: "It is finished."

161
00:06:12,569 --> 00:06:12,869
Done.

162
00:06:13,362 --> 00:06:14,191
He hath done this.

163
00:06:15,161 --> 00:06:16,775
So hold the two ends of the song together.

164
00:06:16,775 --> 00:06:19,838
The cry was real on the cross he truly bore

165
00:06:19,904 --> 00:06:22,158
the forsaking that our sin had earned, in our

166
00:06:22,198 --> 00:06:22,520
place.

167
00:06:23,208 --> 00:06:25,042
And yet the same psalm tells you how it ends:

168
00:06:25,668 --> 00:06:28,465
God did not hide his face, he heard, he raised

169
00:06:28,465 --> 00:06:28,765
him up.

170
00:06:29,312 --> 00:06:31,204
The forsaking fell on him so that it would

171
00:06:31,305 --> 00:06:32,284
never fall on you.

172
00:06:32,954 --> 00:06:34,727
And the wounded hands were spread wide enough

173
00:06:34,767 --> 00:06:36,985
to reach the ends of the earth every kindred,

174
00:06:37,226 --> 00:06:38,553
every nation, everyone far off.

175
00:06:39,702 --> 00:06:40,712
That includes you.

176
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:43,253
You do not have to make yourself worth saving

177
00:06:43,314 --> 00:06:43,688
before you come.

178
00:06:44,584 --> 00:06:46,237
That is the whole point of Psalm twenty-two:

179
00:06:46,984 --> 00:06:49,018
it ends not with what you must do, but with

180
00:06:49,065 --> 00:06:51,660
what he hath done and the congregation it ends

181
00:06:51,741 --> 00:06:53,060
in still has room for you.

182
00:06:53,789 --> 00:06:55,663
The song has been reaching toward you the whole

183
00:06:55,724 --> 00:06:56,024
time.

184
00:06:56,614 --> 00:06:58,208
Come and join the ones who praise him.
